          title: `What a percentile means, with a worked example`,
          body: `Growth charts are built from measurements of many healthy children. The curved lines show the spread of those measurements at each age. The 50th percentile line is the middle: half of the babies in the reference group are above it, half below.\n\nThe Royal College of Paediatrics and Child Health (RCPCH) puts it like this: if your child's height is on the 25th centile, then if you lined up 100 children of the same age and sex in order of height, your child would probably be number 25. The same logic works for weight and head circumference. A baby on the 75th percentile for weight is heavier than roughly 75 of 100 same-age, same-sex babies.\n\nThe American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) stresses that growth percentiles are not like school grades. Coming in at the 10th percentile is no better or worse than the 90th. Some babies are simply smaller or bigger, often because of their parents' build. Doctors sometimes use z-scores instead, especially for measurements outside the printed curves: a z-score of 0 equals the 50th percentile.`,
