          body: `One measurement is a snapshot. Several measurements plotted over weeks and months show the pattern, and the pattern is what your health team is looking at. The AAP advises parents to focus on the rate and trend of growth rather than any specific number.\n\nWeight rarely follows one line exactly, especially in the first year. RCPCH says it is normal for a weight centile to drop by up to one centile space between measurements, while crossing two spaces is less common. The NHS gives the same message: moving across one centile line is common, but if your baby's weight crosses two centile lines, talk to your health visitor. In that case a health professional may re-measure length, ask about feeding and sometimes run tests.\n\nShort illnesses can cause a dip. According to RCPCH, once a child recovers, their weight should go back to the centile it was on within 2 to 3 weeks. The AAP notes that falling across percentile lines over time can be a sign of an underlying condition and should be checked by your pediatrician.`,
